From: Omarchy Mobile <omamobile@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: pmi8994_fg: don't wait for the release worker under the lock

pmi8994_fg_sram_request_access() runs with chip->lock held (from
pmi8994_fg_get_property()) and calls cancel_delayed_work_sync() on
sram_release_access_work, whose worker takes chip->lock first. When the
worker starts while a reader holds the lock, each waits for the other
forever. On a Redmi 4 Prime (PMI8950, msm8953-xiaomi-markw) this
happened within minutes of boot: the pmi8994_fg kworker and every task
reading the battery (upowerd, systemd-logind, a plain cat of capacity)
sat in D state, and the next s2idle failed to freeze them:

  Freezing user space processes failed after 20.003 seconds
  (7 tasks refusing to freeze, wq_busy=0)
  ...
   cancel_delayed_work_sync+0x24/0x3c
   pmi8994_fg_sram_request_access+0x3c/0x2a0
   pmi8994_fg_sram_read+0x50/0x26c
   pmi8994_fg_get_property+0x244/0x458

Only cancel a pending release. A worker that is already running waits
for the lock and releases access once the reader is done, which is
harmless: the next access requests it again.

The release wait also passed MEM_IF_TIMEOUT_MS to secs_to_jiffies(),
so a release that never sees the interface go idle held the lock for
5000 seconds instead of 5. Use msecs_to_jiffies().

---
 drivers/power/supply/pmi8994_fg.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/power/supply/pmi8994_fg.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/pmi8994_fg.c
@@ -196,7 +196,14 @@
 	bool sram_accessible;
 	int ret;
 
-	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&chip->sram_release_access_work);
+	/*
+	 * Callers hold chip->lock, and the release worker takes it, so
+	 * waiting for a running worker here deadlocks: the worker waits
+	 * for the lock and this waits for the worker. Only drop a pending
+	 * release. A worker already running releases access once we drop
+	 * the lock; the next access requests it again.
+	 */
+	cancel_delayed_work(&chip->sram_release_access_work);
 
 	sram_accessible = pmi8994_fg_sram_check_access(chip);
 
@@ -243,7 +250,7 @@
 
 	ret = wait_event_timeout(chip->sram_waitq,
 				 !pmi8994_fg_sram_check_access(chip),
-				 secs_to_jiffies(MEM_IF_TIMEOUT_MS));
+				 msecs_to_jiffies(MEM_IF_TIMEOUT_MS));
 	if (ret <= 0)
 		dev_err(chip->dev, "SRAM release timeout\n");
 }
